{
  "email": "dana.whitfield@brightline.example",
  "reason": "Identify ticket sender, department and title for software request"
}

{
  "query": "PDF editor Company Portal approved software",
  "reason": "Check whether an approved PDF editor is available in the Company Portal"
}

{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Dana, thanks for checking with us before installing anything. We can't install software from a download link sent by a vendor or anyone outside the company. Free installers from unknown sites are a common way malware gets onto laptops. Here's how to get a PDF editor safely:\n\n1. Open the Company Portal (Software Center) on your laptop and search for \"PDF\". If an approved PDF editor is listed, you can install it yourself right away, with no IT help needed.\n2. If there's nothing suitable, reply with the name of the tool you need and what you need it for, such as editing, signing or combining PDFs. We'll start an approval and security review and suggest a vetted option.\n\nOne quick question: have you already downloaded or run that setup.exe file? If you have, please tell us right away so we can check your laptop. If you haven't, please don't open the link. You can delete the message, or use the Report Phishing button in Outlook if anything about it seemed off.",
  "internal_note": "Sender: Dana Whitfield, CEO, Executive (VIP), user_id 1, device BL-LT-0101. She asked us to install a 'free PDF editor' from a vendor-supplied link (http://free-pdf-tools.example/setup.exe). Per KB-027 we don't install software from emailed links. I did not install anything or start any action. Next steps: (1) user checks the Company Portal for an approved PDF editor, (2) if none fits, get the app name, business reason and approval, then send it for security review and escalate per KB-027. I asked whether she downloaded or ran the setup.exe. If she did, treat it as a possible security incident (device check on BL-LT-0101, KB-029) and escalate, given her VIP status. The link may be phishing or malware sent by a vendor. Consider asking security to look at the sending vendor address.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-027",
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
